Relevant columns and their possible roles  

spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info  
• `Sample` : sample (a.k.a. call‑set) identifier – will be joined to the call’s `call_set_name`.  
• `Super_Population` : super‑population code (AFR, AMR, EAS, EUR, SAS) – grouping field.  
• `Super_Population_Description` : human‑readable label – optional for reporting.  

spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants  
• `reference_name` : chromosome; filter out ‘X’, ‘Y’, ‘MT’.  
• `AF` : overall alternate‑allele frequency – used for “common” (AF ≥ 0.05).  
• `call` (REPEATED STRUCT)  
 • `call_set_name` : sample ID, join to `Sample`.  
 • `genotype` : ARRAY<INT64>; any value > 0 ⇒ sample carries an alternate allele.  

Other potentially useful variant‑level population AF columns  
`AFR_AF`, `AMR_AF`, `EUR_AF`, `ASN_AF` – may be consulted if super‑population specific AF is desired.  

Conditions that will be used  
• `reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')` – autosomes only.  
• `AF >= 0.05` – restrict to common variants (or the population‑specific AF columns as needed).  
• `EXISTS(SELECT x FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS x WHERE x>0)` – sample has ≥1 alt allele.  

Below are ten exploratory BigQuery SQL snippets – each limited to 20 rows – that progress from very simple inspection toward the final analytic shape.  
(Replace … with further predicates when you keep iterating.)

```sql
-- 1. Description: list the distinct super‑population codes available.
SELECT DISTINCT `Super_Population`
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info`
WHERE Super_Population IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: peek at allele‑frequency distribution for autosomal variants.
SELECT reference_name, AF
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`
WHERE reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
  AND AF IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: show one exploded row per (variant, sample) to inspect genotype arrays.
SELECT reference_name,
       start,
       call.call_set_name AS sample,
       call.genotype AS gt
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`,
     UNNEST(call) AS call
WHERE reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: only keep sample/variant pairs where the sample carries ≥1 alternate allele.
SELECT reference_name,
       start,
       call.call_set_name AS sample,
       call.genotype AS gt
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants`,
     UNNEST(call) AS call
WHERE reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
  AND EXISTS (SELECT g FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS g WHERE g > 0)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: join the above to sample_info to attach super‑population.
SELECT v.reference_name,
       v.start,
       s.Super_Population,
       call.call_set_name AS sample
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call) AS call
JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
  ON s.Sample = call.call_set_name
WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
  AND EXISTS (SELECT g FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS g WHERE g > 0)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: confirm population size – number of samples in every super‑population.
SELECT Super_Population,
       COUNT(*) AS sample_cnt
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info`
WHERE Super_Population IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY Super_Population
ORDER BY sample_cnt DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: for a single super‑population (e.g., AFR) count carriers per variant.
SELECT v.reference_name,
       v.start,
       COUNT(DISTINCT call.call_set_name) AS n_carriers
FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
     UNNEST(v.call) AS call
JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
  ON s.Sample = call.call_set_name
WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
  AND v.AF >= 0.05
  AND s.Super_Population = 'AFR'
  AND EXISTS (SELECT g FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS g WHERE g > 0)
GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: bucket variants by the # of AFR samples sharing them.
SELECT n_carriers,
       COUNT(*) AS afr_variant_cnt
FROM (
  SELECT v.reference_name,
         v.start,
         COUNT(DISTINCT call.call_set_name) AS n_carriers
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
       UNNEST(v.call) AS call
  JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
    ON s.Sample = call.call_set_name
  WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
    AND v.AF >= 0.05
    AND s.Super_Population = 'AFR'
    AND EXISTS (SELECT g FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS g WHERE g > 0)
  GROUP BY v.reference_name, v.start
)
GROUP BY n_carriers
ORDER BY n_carriers
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: extend to every super‑population in one pass.
SELECT s.Super_Population,
       COUNT(DISTINCT s.Sample) AS pop_size,
       n_carriers,
       COUNT(*) AS variant_cnt
FROM (
  SELECT v.reference_name,
         v.start,
         call.call_set_name,
         s.Super_Population
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
       UNNEST(v.call) AS call
  JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
    ON s.Sample = call.call_set_name
  WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
    AND v.AF >= 0.05
    AND EXISTS (SELECT g FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS g WHERE g > 0)
) AS x
JOIN (
  -- carriers per variant+super‑pop
  SELECT reference_name,
         start,
         Super_Population,
         COUNT(DISTINCT call_set_name) AS n_carriers
  FROM (
    SELECT v.reference_name,
           v.start,
           call.call_set_name,
           s.Super_Population
    FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
         UNNEST(v.call) AS call
    JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
      ON s.Sample = call.call_set_name
    WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
      AND v.AF >= 0.05
      AND EXISTS (SELECT g FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS g WHERE g > 0)
  )
  GROUP BY reference_name, start, Super_Population
) USING(reference_name,start,Super_Population)
GROUP BY s.Super_Population, n_carriers
ORDER BY s.Super_Population, n_carriers
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: final‑shape query – full result (remove LIMIT when ready) with common‑variant flag.
SELECT sp.Super_Population,
       pop_size,
       TRUE  AS is_common_AF_ge_0_05,
       n_carriers AS samples_with_variant,
       COUNT(*)  AS variants_shared_by_that_many
FROM (
  -- total sample counts per super‑pop
  SELECT Super_Population, COUNT(*) AS pop_size
  FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info`
  GROUP BY Super_Population
) AS sp
JOIN (
  -- carriers per variant per super‑pop
  SELECT reference_name,
         start,
         Super_Population,
         COUNT(DISTINCT call_set_name) AS n_carriers
  FROM (
    SELECT v.reference_name,
           v.start,
           call.call_set_name,
           s.Super_Population
    FROM `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.variants` AS v,
         UNNEST(v.call) AS call
    JOIN `spider2-public-data.1000_genomes.sample_info` AS s
      ON s.Sample = call.call_set_name
    WHERE v.reference_name NOT IN ('X','Y','MT')
      AND v.AF >= 0.05
      AND EXISTS (SELECT g FROM UNNEST(call.genotype) AS g WHERE g > 0)
  )
  GROUP BY reference_name, start, Super_Population
) AS vpop
ON sp.Super_Population = vpop.Super_Population
GROUP BY sp.Super_Population, pop_size, n_carriers
ORDER BY sp.Super_Population, n_carriers
LIMIT 20;
```

Each successive query exposes more of the building blocks you will need for the complete analysis while adhering to the requested LIMIT 20 rows.
